# Tuning applied to IDENTITY=own.
#
# Appended to arch/arm64/configs/gki_defconfig right before the build generates
# its .config, so these are the last word on each symbol. NOT applied to `repro`,
# which has to stay an exact copy of the phone's kernel.
#
# Every symbol here is also what scripts/20-verify.sh is allowed to forgive, by
# construction: it reads this file and accepts exactly these symbols. A diff
# outside this list still fails the build.
#
# One entry per reason. If a knob cannot say why it is here, it does not belong.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Debug symbols are not built.
#
# CONFIG_DEBUG_INFO with DWARF4 is ~1 GB of vmlinux nobody ships. It does not
# change the Image at all - build time and artifact size only. This is the one
# change in this file that cannot affect how the phone runs, which is exactly
# why it survived the batch 1 failure.
#
# The two lines after it are not settings: they are the symbols that stop
# existing once DEBUG_INFO is off. They are listed so this file stays a complete
# record of what the build changes.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CONFIG_DEBUG_INFO is not set
# CONFIG_GDB_SCRIPTS is not set
# CONFIG_DEBUG_EFI is not set

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Timer frequency: 300 Hz, the tree's own value.
#
# This was 250 and the reason given was idle cost ("17% fewer timer
# interrupts"). That reason was wrong for this kernel: CONFIG_NO_HZ_IDLE is
# set, so the tick is stopped completely when the CPU is idle. HZ therefore
# costs essentially nothing on standby, and only sets the granularity of
# timers and scheduler ticks while the screen is awake and animating.
#
# At 250 Hz a tick lands every 4.0 ms. Android paces frames at 8.33 ms (120 Hz)
# and 16.7 ms (60 Hz), so a wakeup can be quantised up to 4 ms late - enough to
# miss a frame and be seen as a stutter in an animation. 300 Hz brings that to
# 3.3 ms. This is the one runtime default we changed that can plausibly affect
# how smooth the UI feels, so it is the first thing to flip back if 300 Hz
# drains worse than it feels better.
#
# Revert: change both lines back to 250.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CONFIG_HZ_300=y
CONFIG_HZ=300
# CONFIG_HZ_300 is not set

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. DAMON: give the kernel the ability to monitor user address spaces.
#
# DAMON (Data Access Monitoring, mainline 5.15) is already backported into this
# tree by Google: CONFIG_DAMON, DAMON_PADDR and DAMON_RECLAIM are built. What is
# missing is DAMON_VADDR - the half that can watch *user* memory - and
# DAMON_DBGFS, the interface needed to configure it.
#
# Why this and not MGLRU: DAMON does not replace the LRU. It samples access
# patterns and lets a policy (DAMON_RECLAIM) reclaim pages that are provably
# cold. MGLRU rewrites reclaim, and the one attempt to bring it up half-enabled
# made the phone sluggish; this only watches. Nothing here depends on that.
#
# These two symbols change no behaviour on their own. Nothing is monitored until
# a policy is configured, and configuring it needs debugfs, which needs root.
# That is deliberate: the capability ships now, the experiment happens when we
# can actually read the result.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CONFIG_DAMON_VADDR=y
CONFIG_DAMON_DBGFS=y

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. BBRplus congestion control: build it, do not default to it.
#
# Added by patches/0001-tcp-bbrplus.patch (net/ipv4/tcp_bbrplus.c + Kconfig +
# Makefile). BBRplus is BBR with drain-to-target cycling, tuned for low latency
# and sustained throughput on mobile links; taken from zylhdrXP's GKI fork where
# it ships in release builds.
#
# Building it only makes it available. The default stays BBRv3, so this changes
# nothing until something selects it:
#
#   su -c 'echo bbrplus > /proc/sys/net/ipv4/tcp_congestion_control'
#
# ...which needs root. Same gate as DAMON.
#
# DEFAULT_BBRPLUS is the choice entry the same Kconfig block adds. It is
# deliberately left unset: BBRplus is built and available, but BBRv3 remains the
# default congestion control. The verifier refused this build until the line was
# written down here, which is the gate doing its job.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CONFIG_TCP_CONG_BBRPLUS=y
# CONFIG_DEFAULT_BBRPLUS is not set

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. CAKE: build the scheduler, keep fq_codel as the default queue.
#
# Taken from SuiKernel (5.10.264), the only one of the three kernels dropped in
# Desktop\Arkael\FORK that sets a symbol we do not have. Their config has
# NET_SCH_CAKE=y and DEFAULT_NET_SCH="cake"; ours has the source file sitting
# unused in net/sched/sch_cake.c because the symbol was never enabled.
#
# Only the build half is taken. Their default is cake, and making cake the
# default qdisc sends every wifi packet through a heavier shaper on the CPU -
# a cost we have no measurement for. fq_codel stays default; cake is one line
# away for whoever wants it:
#
#   su -c 'tc qdisc replace dev wlan0 root cake'
#
# No behaviour changes until something selects it, same gate as DAMON and
# BBRplus. See docs/FORK-AUDIT.md for what the other two kernels had and why
# none of it was taken.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CONFIG_NET_SCH_CAKE=y

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. Power-efficient workqueues: tried, and backed out on 2026-11-09.
#
# What it does: workqueues that opt in with WQ_POWER_EFFICIENT - and the two
# system-level ones - stop being pinned to the CPU that queued the work and are
# handed to the scheduler, which can pick a core that is already awake instead
# of waking an idle one.
#
# Why it is off now: the phone stalls for a few seconds on wake when a video app
# (Facebook, TikTok) was left open through sleep, and then recovers by itself.
# That happens on 2142 as well - before any of the twelve upstream fixes - and
# it does not happen on Arkael's kernel, which has this symbol off, like Google's
# GKI defconfig and the phone's own config. We were the only kernel in that
# comparison that set it.
#
# That is the case for reverting it: this option changes *when* unbound work
# runs, not how much power it costs, so it can leave a resume waiting behind a
# worker the scheduler has not placed yet. A stall that heals on its own has
# that shape, and no other line in our config delta can produce a delay rather
# than a loss.
#
# The benefit was never measured here. The "up to 15%" figure is a quote about
# other systems, and the drain experiment that would have justified it was lost
# when the phone went back to a known-good build.
#
# Revert (restore the experiment): change this back to =y.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CONFIG_WQ_POWER_EFFICIENT_DEFAULT is not set

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 7. MGLRU: REVERTED, do not add back without a device test.
#
# It was merged (35a478789) and removed again (4a8a27860). Two configurations,
# two failures, and they bracket the feature:
#
#   b63ed8289  core only, page-table walks disabled at boot: boots and works,
#              then goes sluggish after hours of uptime, because aging advances
#              the generation counter without scanning anything (vmscan.c
#              ~line 3957) and reclaim evicts in allocation order.
#
#   41fac00aa  core plus walks: hangs right after the lock screen, reproducibly,
#              twice. No log was captured, so the mechanism is unknown; the walk
#              is the only difference from a build that boots.
#
# So there is no safe half: the capability bitmap has exactly these two sides.
# The experiment stays addressable as tag mglru-walks-hang and branch
# mglru-experiment. Config symbols below are deliberately absent - the tree no
# longer has mm/lru_gen.c, and asking for LRU_GEN there is a warning, not a knob.
#
# What would change the verdict: a backport whose walk path is tested on a
# vendor 5.10 tree with a serial console, on a spare device.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REVERTED, do not add back: CONFIG_KASAN off.
#
# Reason: the build with KASAN off **hangs this phone at the POCO logo**, with no
# adb and no log. Proven by bisection - the same KernelSU-Next driver with the
# phone's original config boots fine, so the sanitizer removal was the cause.
# Image size difference confirms it: 35,873,484 bytes with KASAN, 35,805,516
# without, while DEBUG_INFO changes nothing in the Image.
#
# Whatever the theoretical cost of running KASAN on this hardware, an unbootable
# kernel costs more. If it is ever revisited, it needs a device-side log (serial
# or last_kmsg) to explain the hang first - not another flash.
#
#   # CONFIG_KASAN is not set
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 8. Diagnostics: taken back out on 2026-11-05.
#
# These four shipped into 1840 and 1906 - daily-driver builds - because they
# were added while chasing the 5.10.273 boot freeze and nobody took them out
# again. Read from 1906's own Image:
#
#   CONFIG_SOFTLOCKUP_DETECTOR=y
#   CONFIG_BOOTPARAM_SOFTLOCKUP_PANIC=y     <- CPU stuck 20s -> panic+reboot
#   CONFIG_BOOTPARAM_HUNG_TASK_PANIC=y      <- task stuck 120s -> panic+reboot
#
# Two reasons that is wrong for a phone someone carries:
#
#   - it reboots itself on a hang instead of recovering, where the previous
#     kernel (Arkael) shipped the detector off and PANIC_TIMEOUT=-1;
#   - the softlockup detector is a per-CPU kthread woken by an hrtimer every
#     4s. Eight CPUs, on the little cluster, and it is a plain kthread, so
#     WQ_POWER_EFFICIENT does not apply: roughly two idle exits per second all
#     night, which is the one thing that keeps a cluster out of deep idle.
#
# PANIC_TIMEOUT stays: with the two panicking BOOTPARAM lines gone it only
# applies to a genuine kernel panic, where rebooting beats lying there dead.
#
# Trade-off, stated plainly: no watchdog means a future freeze leaves no
# pstore record. That is the right trade for a driver; it was the wrong trade
# for a debugging session, and this file had them confused.
#
# The original reasoning is kept below because it was correct for its purpose.
#
# A hang that leaves no log is not a test, it is a coin flip. 41fac00aa froze
# right after the PIN and produced nothing usable: PSTORE, PSTORE_RAM and
# PSTORE_CONSOLE were already compiled in, so the console ring *would* have been
# written to ramoops - but nothing ever declared a lockup, so there was no panic
# to mark it and nothing that stopped the phone from just sitting there.
#
# What was missing:
#
#   # CONFIG_SOFTLOCKUP_DETECTOR is not set   <- a CPU spinning forever was
#                                               never noticed at all
#   CONFIG_BOOTPARAM_HUNG_TASK_PANIC_VALUE=0  <- a task stuck in D-state for
#                                               120s was logged, not acted on
#
# With both panicking, the phone writes the dump into ramoops and reboots, and
# /sys/fs/pstore on the next boot holds the answer. PANIC_TIMEOUT has to be
# positive for that reboot to happen: -1 keeps the kernel sitting in panic, which
# would turn a frozen screen into a permanently frozen screen.
#
# Cost: one watchdog hrtimer per CPU from the softlockup detector, and the
# existing hung-task thread doing the same work it already did. If idle drain
# moves, this is the line that comes back out - it is a diagnostic, not a daily
# driver setting. After a hang has been explained, remove all four.
#
# Reading it back:
#
#   su -c 'ls -l /sys/fs/pstore'
#   su -c 'cat /sys/fs/pstore/console-ramoops*'
#
# If /sys/fs/pstore is empty and dmesg has no "ramoops" line, the device reserves
# no region for it and this buys nothing - check that before trusting it.
#
# LOCKUP_DETECTOR is listed because SOFTLOCKUP_DETECTOR *selects* it: it is not
# a choice, it is the dependency pulled in behind it. Without a line here the
# verifier found it in the built config, could not find it in this file, and
# refused the build - which is the guard working as intended, since a symbol
# nobody asked for is exactly what a config allowlist exists to catch.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CONFIG_LOCKUP_DETECTOR is not set
# CONFIG_SOFTLOCKUP_DETECTOR is not set
# CONFIG_BOOTPARAM_SOFTLOCKUP_PANIC is not set
# CONFIG_BOOTPARAM_HUNG_TASK_PANIC is not set
CONFIG_PANIC_TIMEOUT=5
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 9. KSM: compiled in for a measured experiment, 2026-11-09.
#
# What this section said before was reasoning without a measurement: "the
# vendor does not build it either, and zram already saves that memory more
# cheaply". The measurement now exists, and it says the opposite of "already
# saved cheaply":
#
#   - 19.5 minutes with the screen off: 30,764 pages/min swapped out,
#     19,357 in, 19,802 major faults/min, zram grew 5,813 -> 6,019 MB;
#   - the phone holds ~6 GB of an 8 GB zram device while nominally idle;
#   - CPU is 915 of ~1600 mAh of the drain, and that CPU is this churn.
#
# So the question is worth one build: does deduplicating identical pages across
# the 10-20 resident apps free enough memory to reduce that churn by more than
# the scanner costs? That is what this experiment measures, nothing else.
#
# Compiled in but NOT started: ksmd sleeps until something writes 1 to
# /sys/kernel/mm/ksm/run, so flashing this kernel changes no behaviour by
# itself. Start by hand, measure against the numbers above, stop by writing 0.
# If it loses, set this back to "is not set".
#
# Revert: put back `# CONFIG_KSM is not set`.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CONFIG_KSM=y
